NAME
    dump - write a raw memory range to a file

SYNOPSIS
    dump <space> <addr> <len> <file>

DESCRIPTION
    Reads <len> bytes (hex) from memory space <space> starting at hex
    <addr> and writes them verbatim with no header or metadata to <file>.
    Refuses spaces backed by live hardware registers with read side
    effects (such as CpuBus); use plain spaces such as WRAM, VRAM, or ROM.

    Relative <file> paths resolve under /Logs/<CoreName>/; paths starting
    with '/' resolve from the shell sandbox root (see hier(7)). Paths that
    escape the sandbox root are refused. Parent directories inside the
    sandbox are created automatically.

EXAMPLES
    dump WRAM 0 2000 wram.bin
    dump WRAM 0 2000 /Logs/wram.bin

EXIT STATUS
    0   Bytes dumped (or usage/refusal message printed).
    1   Path outside sandbox, no target attached, invalid hex argument,
        or I/O or session service fault.

SEE ALSO
    load(1), mem(1), spaces(1), snapshot(1), hier(7)
